'''wood-offering†''' (wu̇d′of″ėr-ing), ''n.'' Wood burnt on the altar.
{{smaller block|We cast the lots among the priests, the Levites, and the people for the ''wood offering''. Neh. x. 34.}}

'''wood-of-the-holy-cross†''', ''n.'' [Trans. of L. ''lignum sanctæ crucis.''] A name once given to the mistletoe, ''Viscum album'', from its reputed virtue in helping the infirmities of old age. ''Treas. of Bot.''

'''wood-oil''' (wu̇d′oil), ''n.'' '''1.''' See ''gurjun.''—'''2.''' Same as ''tung-oil.''—'''3.''' A product of the satinwood, ''Chloroxylon Swietenia.''

'''wood-opal''' (wu̇d′ō″pạl), ''n.'' Silicified wood; opalized wood. {{smaller|It is found in great abundance in many parts of the world, but especially in the auriferous gravels of the Sierra Nevada of California, where extensive forests have been exposed by hydraulic mining, in which the trunks of the trees have been converted into amorphous silica, or opal, which usually contains a small percentage of water, although this is not considered as being essential to its composition. Also called ''xylopal.'' See ''fossil wood'' (under ''wood''<sup>1</sup>), and ''silicify.''}}

'''wood-owl''' (wu̇d′oul), ''n.'' The European tawny or brown owl, ''Syrnium aluco'', or a similar species, as the barred owl of the United States. {{smaller|They are earless owls, of medium to large size, the species of which are numerous and live in the woods of most parts}}

